  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nSatellite this afternoon depicts a broad region of clouds west of \nnorthern Baja streaming through a branch of the subtropical jet \nstream toward Socal. Radar is beginning to pick up on light rain \nwest of San Clemente Island, set to arrive at the coast during the \nlater afternoon hours, moving inland through the evening. High \nresolution models now agree that tonight's rainfall will be \nwidespread and light region-wide, perhaps more intermittent in the \nhigh desert, with some brief pockets of moderate to heavy rainfall, \nespecially for coastal areas and for San Diego County. Widespread \nrain turns into scattered showers and wrap up in the desert valleys \nSunday morning. Showers become increasingly more isolated through \nthe afternoon, tapering off for northern areas by Sunday evening, \nleaving behind a few isolated showers in San Diego County through \nSunday night. \n\nRain totals for this system will generally be higher for southern \nareas; SD County up to the mountains is expecting 0.75-1\" of rain \nwith locally high end amounts above 1.5\". Orange County and the \nInland Empire can expect 0.3-0.6\" (more south and along foothills), \nwith slightly higher totals in adjacent mountains. The deserts can \ngenerally expect 0.10-0.40\" (more south) of generally light rain. \nGiven that the system is bringing along subtropical moisture, the \nwarm sector will bring snow levels above 10,000 feet tonight before \ndropping back to 7,500-8,000 ft. A dusting is possible above 8,000 \nfeet over the highest passes and peaks, otherwise, no snow is \nexpected for any mountain communities.\n\nAs the upper level trough passes to our north Sunday afternoon, \nthere will be a brief uptick in west/northwest winds over the \nwaters, mountain ridges and desert slopes, with gusts 25-40 mph, \nweakening overnight into Monday.\n\n\nAs the storm exits the region, low temperatures will drop \nMonday/Tuesday morning to the upper-40s inland, upper 30s in the \nhigh desert and down into the low 30s and 20s in elevated mountain \nvalleys. Patchy frost is possible in the high desert. \n\nNext week, a series of weak, fast-moving ridges and troughs will \nkeep temperatures fluctuating, though gradually increasing by late \nweek to the low to mid-80s regionwide for lower elevations. These \nare near average temperatures inland but above average for coastal \nareas. Generally expecting sunny skies with a few passing clouds \nTuesday and Wednesday.",
